import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class VulnerableCode {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Read file
        File file = new File("example.txt");
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(file);

        // Read all lines
        List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();
        while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
            lines.add(scanner.nextLine());
        }
        scanner.close();

        // Print first 5 lines
        for (int i = 0; i < 5 && i < lines.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println(lines.get(i));
        }
    }
}
```

This code is **vulnerable** because it doesn't properly handle file operations. The `Scanner` should be closed after use, and file paths should be validated.

[code snippet 2]
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class FixedCode {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Read file with proper error handling
        File file = new File("example.txt");

        try {
            // Check if file exists
            if (!file.exists()) {
                System.out.println("File not found");
                return;
            }

            // Read file line by line
            Scanner scanner = new Scanner(file);
            List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();

            while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
                lines.add(scanner.nextLine());
            }
            scanner.close();

            // Print first 5 lines
            for (int i = 0; i < 5 && i < lines.size(); i++) {
                System.out.println(lines.get(i));
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error reading file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

The fix includes:
1. Proper file existence check
2. Try-catch block for IOException
3. Proper closing of Scanner
4. Validation of file paths

